.\" Manual page for zipfs.
.\" The option list between the two markers below comes from SETTINGS in
.\" src/cli.rs. Do not write in it by hand: run 'UPDATE_MAN=1 cargo test'
.\" instead. A test writes the same thing and fails where this is behind.
.\" The prose around the markers is written by hand.
.TH ZIPFS 1 "2026-09-07" "zipfs" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
zipfs \- mount a zip archive as a read\-only filesystem
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B zipfs
.RI [ OPTIONS ]
.I ARCHIVE MOUNTPOINT
.br
.B mount \-t fuse.zipfs
.RI [ \-o
.IR OPT [, OPT ...]]
.I ARCHIVE MOUNTPOINT
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B zipfs
mounts the zip archive
.I ARCHIVE
at
.I MOUNTPOINT
through FUSE. The files in the archive are then read like any other files.
Nothing is written to disk: each read decompresses only the bytes it needs,
and the kernel keeps the pages it has already seen.
.PP
The mount is read\-only. Stored, deflate and zstd entries can be read.
Entries that are encrypted, or that use another compression method, are
counted at mount time and cannot be read.
.PP
The archive must not change while it is mounted. Its contents are treated as
fixed, which is what lets the kernel cache pages and metadata indefinitely.
.PP
Without
.B \-\-foreground
the process forks when the mount is ready, and the first process exits. A
script can therefore mount and then read, with no wait in between.
.SH OPTIONS
Each option below has two spellings: the long option shown here, and the same
name without the dashes in an
.B \-o
list. A list accepts dashes or underscores in a name.
.\" BEGIN OPTIONS: written by cli.rs, not by hand
.TP
.BI \-\-threads " N"
FUSE worker threads [default: cores, up to 8].
.TP
.B \-\-no\-verify
Skip the CRC32 check on entries that are read to the end. The alias \fBnoverify\fR names the same thing.
.TP
.B \-\-verify
Check entries against their CRC32 [default].
.TP
.BI \-\-source\-buffer\-size " N"
Compressed bytes each decoder buffers [default: 524288].
.TP
.BI \-\-decoders\-per\-file " N"
Idle decoders one open file may keep [default: 4].
.TP
.BI \-\-max\-retained\-decoders " N"
Idle decoders the mount may keep [default: 64].
.TP
.BI \-\-uid " ID"
Owner reported for every file [default: caller].
.TP
.BI \-\-gid " ID"
Group reported for every file [default: caller].
.TP
.BI \-\-file\-mode " MODE"
Permissions for files whose entry records none [default: 644].
.TP
.BI \-\-dir\-mode " MODE"
Permissions for directories whose entry records none [default: 755].
.TP
.BI \-\-umask " MASK"
Bits to take away from both modes above.
.TP
.BI \-\-fmask " MASK"
Bits to take away from the file mode.
.TP
.BI \-\-dmask " MASK"
Bits to take away from the directory mode.
.TP
.BI \-\-attr\-ttl " SECS"
How long the kernel may cache metadata [default: 31536000].
.TP
.B \-\-allow\-other
Let other users see the mount.
.TP
.B \-\-allow\-root
Let root see the mount.
.TP
.B \-\-auto\-unmount
Unmount if this process dies; needs \-\-allow\-other or \-\-allow\-root.
.\" END OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-o " OPT" [, OPT ...]
Any option above, without the dashes, as
.BR mount (8)
and
.BR fstab (5)
spell it. Options that belong to the kernel or to
.BR mount (8),
such as
.BR ro " and " noatime ,
are accepted and passed on.
.TP
.BR \-f ", " \-\-foreground
Stay in the foreground and keep logging to standard error
.TP
.B \-s
Serve with one thread, the same as
.BR \-\-threads " 1"
.TP
.BR \-v ", " \-\-verbose
Log more. Repeat for debug and trace.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Print the help
.TP
.BR \-V ", " \-\-version
Print the version
.SH MOUNT HELPER
Installed as
.IR /usr/sbin/mount.fuse.zipfs ,
the program is what
.BR mount (8)
starts for the type
.BR fuse.zipfs .
An fstab line then reads:
.PP
.RS
.EX
/srv/data.zip  /mnt/data  fuse.zipfs  ro,noauto,allow_other  0 0
.EE
.RE
.PP
Started under a name that begins with
.BR mount. ,
the short options take their
.BR mount (8)
meanings instead:
.B \-s
tolerates unknown options,
.B \-f
does everything but the mount,
.B \-n
is accepted and does nothing, and
.B \-t
names the type. Use
.B \-\-foreground
and
.B \-\-threads
to reach what
.B \-f
and
.B \-s
mean otherwise.
.SH EXAMPLES
Mount an archive, read it, and unmount it:
.PP
.RS
.EX
zipfs archive.zip /mnt/archive
ls /mnt/archive
fusermount3 \-u /mnt/archive
.EE
.RE
.PP
Mount with 4 threads, and let other users read the files:
.PP
.RS
.EX
zipfs \-\-threads 4 \-\-allow\-other archive.zip /mnt/archive
.EE
.RE
.PP
A systemd mount unit. The name of the file must agree with the mount point:
.I /mnt/archive
becomes
.IR mnt\-archive.mount .
.PP
.RS
.EX
[Unit]
Description=Zip archive at /mnt/archive

[Mount]
What=/srv/archive.zip
Where=/mnt/archive
Type=fuse.zipfs
Options=ro,nosuid,nodev,allow_other,threads=4

[Install]
WantedBy=multi\-user.target
.EE
.RE
.PP
Put the file in
.IR /etc/systemd/system/ ,
then:
.PP
.RS
.EX
systemctl daemon\-reload
systemctl start mnt\-archive.mount
.EE
.RE
.PP
The package installs this example at
.IR /usr/share/doc/zipfs/examples/mnt\-archive.mount .
.SH EXIT STATUS
.B zipfs
exits with 0 when the mount is ready, and with 1 when it is not. Where the
process forks, the code is the child's verdict: the first process waits until
the child serves the mount, or reports why it cannot.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /usr/bin/zipfs
The program.
.TP
.I /usr/sbin/mount.fuse.zipfs
The name
.BR mount (8)
starts for the type
.BR fuse.zipfs .
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR mount (8),
.BR fstab (5),
.BR fusermount3 (1),
.BR systemd.mount (5)
.SH BUGS
Report bugs at
.UR https://github.com/nickbabcock/zipfs/issues
.UE .
